<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Uglich</h1><p class="st-meta">1218 CE – 1328 CE (approx.) · capital Uglich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage principality on the upper Volga, split off from Rostov and bought by Ivan Kalita of Moscow.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> lands. In 1218 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-of-rostov">Konstantin Vsevolodovich of Rostov</a> gave it to his son Vladimir, who became the first prince of a separate Uglich line.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was small, with its wealth drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> trade and farming. It was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1238. Its prince Roman Vladimirovich, who died in 1285, was later venerated as a saint for his charity to the poor. Like other small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> of the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> depended on the grand princes and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After its line declined, the principality was acquired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I Kalita</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> around 1328, one of his famous purchases of land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> later became the scene of the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-uglich">Tsarevich Dmitry</a> in 1591.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-uglich.jpg" alt="Principality of Uglich" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Mike1979 Russia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feodorovskaya_Church_Bogoyavlensky_Monastery_Uglich_2023-07-22_7446.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1218 CE – 1328 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Uglich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vladimir Konstantinovich, Roman Vladimirovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uglich Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1273&amp;focus=principality-of-uglich" aria-label="Principality of Uglich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5502 230 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Uglich lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5822 486L5878 523M5822 486L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5822 486h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5822 486h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1273&amp;focus=principality-of-uglich">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
